window.HS_BG = {
  folksongs: "Blasting mats are industrial objects: old truck tyres cut into strips and tied with steel cable, made to stop flying rock during blasting work. Prince found them in rural upstate New York, where he has lived and worked since the 1990s. Most of his sculpture from these years is built from that landscape's leftovers: car bonnets, tyres and body filler.",
  bigwheel: "Jafa first made his name as a cinematographer. He shot Julie Dash's Daughters of the Dust (1991), which won the cinematography award at Sundance in 1992. Objects like this came later, after 2016, when Love Is the Message made him a star of the art world. The 'II' marks it as one of a series of chained giant wheels.",
  sunsets: "From 1977 Prince photographed adverts out of magazines and presented the new prints as his own work, a method he called 'rephotography'. It made him a key figure of the so-called Pictures Generation, alongside Cindy Sherman, Sherrie Levine and Barbara Kruger: artists who treated mass-media images as raw material.",
  lovemessage: "Jafa premiered it at Gavin Brown's Enterprise in New York in November 2016, the week of Donald Trump's first election. The title combines MFSB's disco hit 'Love Is the Message', the theme of Soul Train, with James Tiptree Jr.'s 1973 science-fiction story 'Love Is the Plan the Plan Is Death'. In June 2020, after George Floyd's murder, several museums streamed it free online, among them the Hirshhorn in Washington.",
  girlfriend: "The source was the readers' pages of American biker magazines, where men sent in photos of girlfriends posing with their motorcycles. Prince began rephotographing them in 1993. The poses, the cropping and the amateur flash are all kept; only the scale and the context change.",
  loml: "Greg Tate (1957–2021) was one of the most important writers on Black culture in the US. He wrote for The Village Voice for decades, co-founded the Black Rock Coalition in 1985 and led the band Burnt Sugar. In 2003 he edited the anthology Everything but the Burden, which included Jafa's essay 'My Black Death'.",
  viriconium: "Earlier versions of this cutout crowd were shown under the title __ Array. Viriconium is the decaying city in M. John Harrison's fantasy books, beginning with The Pastel City (1971): a place where the past lies around in fragments that nobody fully understands, like Jafa's image archive.",
  chopper: "The chopper, a stripped-down bike with stretched front forks, became a symbol of freedom through the 1969 film Easy Rider. Biker culture runs through Prince's work: the Girlfriends downstairs, magazine collections, jokes and drawings.",
  entertainers: "Prince worked in the tear-sheet department at Time-Life, cutting articles out of magazines for the company's writers. The leftover advertising pages gave him the idea of rephotography. The Entertainers belong to the same early years, when he first used other people's portraits as stand-ins for himself.",
  duffy: "In 1836 a Black trans woman known as Mary Jones was arrested in New York. The trial became a press sensation, and a lithograph sold around the city mocked her as 'The Man-Monster'. She is one of the earliest documented trans people in US history. Jafa restages the picture with himself in the role.",
  cowboy: "In 1980 Prince began cropping the cowboys out of Marlboro cigarette ads and rephotographing them, his most famous series. In 2005 one of these photographs sold at Christie's for about $1.2 million, the first photograph to pass the million-dollar mark at auction. The small sculpture here turns that macho icon into a child in costume.",
  dekooning: "Willem de Kooning's Woman paintings, especially Woman I (1950–52, now at MoMA), shocked their first audiences with their aggressive, grinning female figures. Prince treats them as a found image like any other, and the violence of the brushwork becomes a game with gender.",
  whitealbum: "The White Album was commissioned by the Berkeley Art Museum (BAMPFA) and premiered there in December 2018. A few months later it won Jafa the Golden Lion for best artist at the 2019 Venice Biennale. The title borrows from the Beatles, whose song 'Helter Skelter' is on their White Album; this exhibition takes its name from the same song.",
  iknowthisguy: "Prince began painting jokes in the mid-1980s: old borscht-belt one-liners about psychiatrists, wives and money, often with no picture at all. Repeated in different colours and sizes, the punchline stops being funny and starts to look like a symptom.",
  idontcare: "The three men murdered in Duluth on 15 June 1920 were Elias Clayton, Elmer Jackson and Isaac McGhie. A memorial to them opened in Duluth in 2003. Lynching photographs were often sold as postcards; Jafa reproduces the image to show how such pictures were made to circulate.",
  lerage: "The Incredible Hulk was created by Stan Lee and Jack Kirby and first appeared in May 1962, while the civil rights movement was reaching its peak. The comic was never explicitly about race, but Jafa, a lifelong comics reader, sees in the Hulk's trapped fury a portrait of Black anger in America.",
  protest: "The 'I AM A MAN' signs come from the Memphis sanitation workers' strike of February–April 1968, after two workers were crushed in a garbage truck. Martin Luther King Jr. came to Memphis to support the strikers and was assassinated there on 4 April 1968. Ernest Withers's photograph of the marchers with the signs became one of the defining images of the era.",
  summerrain: "Apocalypse Now (1979) opens with helicopters and napalm over a palm forest while The Doors' 'The End' (1967) plays. Prince grew up in the years of the Vietnam War protests, and in Summer Rain the blank protest sign is finally filled, with war.",
  exslave: "The photograph, often called 'The Scourged Back', was taken in 1863 in Louisiana by the studio McPherson & Oliver. Harper's Weekly printed an engraving of it on 4 July 1863 with the story of the man, who escaped to Union lines. It became the most widely seen image of slavery's violence during the Civil War.",
  nirvana: "Painting is a newer part of Jafa's practice; this canvas was made in 2025 with the help of the painter Chen Hongjin. Next to the scarred back, the near-black surface reads as a place of rest, or of oblivion.",
  americanplace: "The title echoes An American Place, the New York gallery Alfred Stieglitz ran from 1929 to 1946 to show American modern art. Stieglitz's own photograph hangs in the next room.",
  bengazarra: "Taxi Driver was written by Paul Schrader and directed by Martin Scorsese. In the finished film the pimp Sport is played by Harvey Keitel, a white actor; the script's Black characters were rewritten for fear of setting off racial violence in cinemas. Jafa showed the work in New York in 2024.",
  stieglitz: "Stieglitz (1864–1946) fought for photography to be accepted as art. Spiritual America (1923) is a close-up of a gelded horse's harness: a dark joke about a country that tames its own energy. In 1983 Prince borrowed the title for one of his most contested works and for a short-lived storefront gallery in New York.",
  jafauntitled: "Jafa has kept image notebooks since the 1980s: binders of photocopies and cut-outs from magazines, books and later the internet. Many of his films and prints are built directly from them; this wall shows the method in its most open form.",
  publicity: "Prince is a serious collector of first editions (especially Beat writers like Kerouac and Burroughs), rock memorabilia and signed photographs. In his hands the collection itself becomes the work.",
  tireplanter: "Tyre planters, cut and turned inside out and often painted white, are a typical sight in the front yards of rural America. Prince sees them as folk sculpture, made by people who would never call it art.",
  slopex: "SloPEX is the slowed-down version of APEX (2013), an eight-minute video of more than 800 images from Jafa's archive cut to a techno track. The slower pace turns the flicker into a sequence you can actually read.",
  bitches: "Prince called these grids 'gangs'. He made them in the mid-1980s by laying slides of one subject side by side, inspired by how magazines group look-alike photos on a page.",
  criminals: "The gesture of hiding your face from the camera is the same for a celebrity leaving a restaurant and a suspect leaving a courthouse. Prince's grid shows the gesture, not the people.",
  twothreeworlds: "The sunset is the most photographed subject in the world and the most typical postcard. Repeated twelve times it becomes an abstract pattern, close to the solar discs in Jafa's films.",
  mickey: "Mickey Mouse first appeared in Steamboat Willie (1928). Historians have long traced his look and manner to the blackface minstrel shows of the 19th and early 20th centuries. That earliest version of Mickey entered the US public domain on 1 January 2024.",
  abstractthreat: "From the early 2000s Prince made 'hoods': car bonnets cast or mounted on the wall and smoothed with body filler, a cross between muscle cars and abstract painting. Upstate New York, where he lives, supplied the material.",
  graduation: "The figure comes from Patrick Cariou's photo book Yes Rasta (2000). Prince's Canal Zone paintings were shown at Gagosian in New York in 2008. Cariou sued; a lower court ruled against Prince in 2011, but in 2013 the appeals court found that 25 of the 30 works were fair use.",
  itsallover: "Like Graduation, this canvas belongs to the Canal Zone series, named after the Panama Canal Zone, where Prince was born in 1949. The mix of nudes, guitars and Rastafarians is Prince's picture of an imaginary lawless country.",
  face: "Prince often paints directly onto used silkscreen frames, so traces of earlier jokes and images show through. The four ovals are a joke too: the shortest possible way to draw a face.",
  akingdon: "The video runs 1 hour 41 minutes and belongs to the Brooklyn Museum. Jafa premiered it in 2018 at Gavin Brown's Enterprise in Harlem. In 2020 MoMA streamed it online with a text by Jafa and the curator T. Jean Lax, as a 'rehearsal for communion' while churches were closed.",
  apex: "APEX runs just over eight minutes. Its images flash past to a hard techno track, so fast that only a few can be recognised. It is the work that SloPEX later slows down.",
  dreams: "The film premiered in 2013, the 50th anniversary of the March on Washington and King's 'I Have a Dream' speech. Instead of the speech it gathers conversations with Black artists and thinkers about what has changed since.",
  aghdra: "AGHDRA (2021) was Jafa's first major video after The White Album. It is computer-generated: a black, glittering sea under a burning sun, with almost no recognisable images. It premiered in New York at the reopened Gavin Brown's Enterprise space on 127th Street.",
  cowboys80s: "The source was the Marlboro campaign, built by the agency Leo Burnett from the 1950s on. Prince simply cut away the text. The image was the same, but without the brand it read as a picture of the American myth itself.",
  nurses: "In 2008 Marc Jacobs used Prince's nurses and jokes for Louis Vuitton's spring collection: the nurses walked the runway in masks. Earlier, in 2004, Sonic Youth had put a Nurse painting on the cover of Sonic Nurse.",
  canalzone: "The case Cariou v. Prince became the standard reference for 'transformative use' in US copyright law until the Supreme Court's 2023 Warhol decision narrowed the idea.",
  newportraits: "New Portraits was first shown at Gagosian in New York in 2014. The works sold for large sums, while the people in the photographs, and the photographers, were paid nothing. The 2024 judgments closed the case."
};

